[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Static modeling depicts the process as inputs transformed to outputs, while dynamic modeling enacts the process so you can see how intermediate and final products are transformed over time. Dynamic modeling enables us to simulate the process and make changes before resources are actually expended, helping to evaluate effects on effort, schedule, and other outcomes.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A static model depicts the process, showing that the inputs are transformed to outputs.A dynamic model enacts the process, so the user can see how intermediate and final products are transformed over time.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "This dynamic process view enables us  to  simulate  the  process  and  make  changes  before  the  resources  are  actually expended. For example, we can use a dynamic process model to help us decide how many testers we need or when we must initiate testing in order to finish on schedule.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "bullet_intro",
    "segment": "Evaluation of a process modeling tool using the five properties identified by Curtis, Kellner, and Over (1992):",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "There are many process modeling tools and techniques, and researchers continue to work to determine which ones are most appropriate for a given situation. But there are some characteristics that are helpful, regardless of technique. Curtis, Kellner, and Over (1992) have identified five categories of desirable properties:",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Facilitates human understanding and communication. The technique should be able to represent the process in a form that most customers and developers can understand, encouraging communication about the process and agreement on its form and improvements. The technique should include sufficient information to allow one or more people to actually perform the process.And the model or tool should form a basis for training.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Facilitates  human understanding and communication. The technique should be able to represent the process in a form that most customers and developers can understand, encouraging communication about the process and agreement on its form and improvements. The technique should include sufficient information to allow one or more people to actually perform the process.And the model or tool should form a basis for training.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Supports process improvement. The technique should identify the essential components of a development or maintenance process. It should allow reuse of processes or  subprocesses  on  subsequent  projects, compare  alternatives, and  estimate  the impact of changes before the process is actually put into practice. Similarly, the technique should assist in selecting tools and techniques for the process, in encouraging organizational learning, and in supporting continuing evolution of the process.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Supports  process  improvement. The technique should identify the essential components of a development or maintenance process. It should allow reuse of processes or  subprocesses  on  subsequent  projects, compare  alternatives, and  estimate  the impact of changes before the process is actually put into practice. Similarly, the technique should assist in selecting tools and techniques for the process, in encouraging organizational learning, and in supporting continuing evolution of the process.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Supports process management. The technique should  allow  the  process  to  be  project-specific. Then, developers and customers should be able to reason about attributes of software creation or evolution. The technique should also support planning and forecasting, monitoring and managing the process, and measuring key process characteristics.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Supports  process  management. The  technique  should  allow  the  process  to  be  project-specific. Then, developers and customers should be able to reason about attributes of software creation or evolution. The technique should also support planning and forecasting, monitoring and managing the process, and measuring key process characteristics.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Provides automated guidance in performing the process. The technique should define all or part of the software development environment, provide guidance and suggestions, and retain reusable process representations for later use.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Provides automated guidance in performing the process. The technique should define all or part of the software development environment, provide guidance and suggestions, and retain reusable process representations for later use.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Supports automated process execution. The technique should automate all or part of the  process, support  cooperative work, capture relevant measurement data, and enforce rules to ensure process integrity.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Supports automated process execution. The technique should automate all or part of the  process, support  cooperative work, capture relevant measurement data, and enforce rules to ensure process integrity.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Illustrative example: using a tool to check a set of code components. If size or structure exceeds predefined limits, the developers can be notified before testing begins, and some components may be reviewed and perhaps redesigned.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "For example, a tool can check a set of code components, evaluating their size and structure. If size or structure exceeds predefined limits, the developers can be notified before testing begins, and some components may be reviewed and perhaps redesigned.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Prescriptive (or normative) process models are prescriptions for the way software development should progress, while descriptive process models are descriptions of the way software development is done in actuality.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Some are prescriptions for  the  way  software  development  should  progress, and  others  are descriptions of the way software development is done in actuality.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The purpose of a prescriptive model is to guide and standardize how development should occur, whereas a descriptive model aims to capture and reflect how development is actually performed, enabling the team to understand gaps between the ideal and the real process.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Building a process  model  and  discussing  its  subprocesses  help  the  team  understand  the  gap between what should be and what is.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "A degree-earning process (bachelor's, master's, or PhD) can be described as a work breakdown structure, because it depicts the project as a set of discrete pieces of work.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Analysis of this kind is sometimes described as generating a work breakdown structure for a given project, because it depicts the project as a set of discrete pieces of work.",
                "img_id": null
            }
            ,
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We can describe each activity with four parameters: the precursor, duration, due date, and endpoint.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "In constructing the degree process as a WBS, you would identify discrete activities (e.g., course enrollment, completing required coursework, passing exams, conducting research, writing a thesis or culminating project, defending the thesis, and completing graduation requirements) each with precursors, durations, due dates, and endpoints (milestones or deliverables). The activity graph would illustrate dependencies with milestones as nodes and activities as the connecting lines.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "the nodes of the graph are the project milestones, and the lines linking the nodes represent the activities involved.",
                "img_id": "image_0_1"
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Signifying that the activity has ended, the endpoint is usually a milestone or deliverable.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "To identify the critical path, list precursors for each activity, compute earliest start times, latest start times, and slack, then determine the path with zero slack.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "For example, the number corresponding to each edge of the graph indicates the number of days required to complete the activity represented by that branch. For each activity, list its precursors and compute the earliest start time, the latest start time, and the slack. Then, identify the critical path.",
                "img_id": "image_0_1"
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We can illustrate the relationships among activities by using these parameters. In particular, we can draw an activity graph to depict the dependencies; the nodes of the graph are the project milestones, and the lines linking the nodes represent the activities involved.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Organizational structure: Projects with high certainty, stability, uniformity, and repetition are suggested to be managed best with a hierarchical organizational structure, such as a chief programmer team, which stresses rules, specialization, formality, and a clear definition of organizational hierarchy. In contrast, when there is a lot of uncertainty, a more democratic approach with participation in decision making, a loosely defined hierarchy, and open communication can be effective.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "A National Science Foundation (1983) investigation found that projects with a high degree  of  certainty, stability, uniformity, and  repetition  can  be  accomplished  more effectively  by  a  hierarchical  organizational  structure  such  as  the  chief  programmer team.These projects require little communication among project members, so they are well-suited to an organization that stresses rules, specialization, formality, and a clear definition of organizational hierarchy.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "On the other hand, when there is much uncertainty involved in a project, a more democratic approach may be better. For example, if the requirements may change as development proceeds, the project has a degree of uncertainty. Likewise, suppose your customer is building a new piece of hardware to interface with a system; if the exact specification of the hardware is not yet known, then the level of uncertainty is high. Here, participation in decision making, a loosely defined hierarchy, and the encouragement of open communication can be effective.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Working styles of coworkers: Kai is a rational extrovert who prioritizes efficiency and makes decisions quickly, needing only the bottom line and not supporting every option with extensive explanations. Marcel is a rational introvert who values thorough information, is highly reputable as a worker, and prefers to gather all relevant information before making a presentation. David is an intuitive extrovert who trusts his intuition, tends to share new ideas openly, enjoys interaction, and works well in collaborative environments.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Kai, a rational extrovert, judges her colleagues by the results they produce. When making a decision, her top priority is efficiency. Thus, she wants to know only the bottom line. She examines her options and their probable effects, but she does not need to see documents or hear explanations supporting each option. If her time is wasted or her efficiency is hampered in some way, she asserts her authority to regain control of the situation.Thus, Kai is good at making sound decisions quickly.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Marcel, a rational introvert, is very different from his colleague Kai. He judges his peers by how busy they are, and he has little tolerance for those who appear not to be working hard all the time. He is a good worker, admired for the energy he devotes to his work. His reputation as a good worker is very important to him, and he prides himself on being accurate and thorough. He does not like to make decisions without complete information. When asked to make a presentation, Marcel does so only after gathering all relevant information on the subject.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Marcel shares an office with David, an intuitive extrovert. Whereas Marcel will not make a decision without complete knowledge of the situation, David prefers to follow his feelings. Often, he will trust his intuition about a problem, basing his decision on professional judgment rather than a slow, careful analysis of the information at hand. Since he is assertive, David tends to tell the others on his project about his new ideas. He is creative, and he enjoys when others recognize his ideas. David likes to work in an environment where there is a great deal of interaction among the staff members.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Advantages and balance: The material suggests that the two types of organizational structure can be combined where appropriate, enabling settings such as developers working on subsystems within a hierarchical framework or self-imposed hierarchy within a loosely structured team for major testing decisions. It also notes that a balance between structure and creativity is important for good project management, preventing excessive focus on either functionality or deadlines and scope.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The two types of organizational structure can be combined, where appropriate. For instance, programmers may be asked to develop a subsystem on their own, using an egoless approach within a hierarchical structure. Or the test team of a loosely structured project may impose a hierarchical structure on itself and designate one person to be responsible for all major testing decisions.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The experts in Kunde's article claim that good project management means finding a balance between structure and creativity. Left to their own devices, the software developers will focus only on functionality and creativity, disregarding deadlines and the scope of the specification.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Sidebar 3.2 describes the need to balance structure with creativity.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

[
    {
        "role": "bullet_intro",
        "segment": "Two techniques for predicting effort:",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Many effort-estimation methods rely on expert judgment.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Expert judgment (including Delphi): use human experts to make initial estimates, possibly using analogy from similar projects, and, in the Delphi variation, have experts provide predictions independently and iteratively revise toward consensus.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Many effort-estimation methods rely on expert judgment.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The Delphi technique makes use of expert judgment in a different way. Experts are asked to make individual predictions secretly, based on their expertise and using whatever process they choose. Then, the average estimate is calculated and presented to the group. Each expert has the opportunity to revise his or her estimate, if desired. The process is repeated until no expert wants to revise.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Algorithmic / model-based estimation: use formal models (e.g., COCOMO or similar) with formulas based on past experience to allocate effort across the software life cycle, and update the model as new data about practices and projects become available.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Some effort and cost models use formulas based on past experience to apportion the effort across the software development life cycle.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "When you are building your own database to support estimation in your organization, it is important to record not only how much effort is expended on a project, but also who is doing it and for what activity.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Expert judgment suffers not only from variability and subjectivity, but also from dependence on current data. The data on which an expert judgment model is based must reflect current practices, so they must be updated often. Moreover, most expert judgment techniques are simplistic, neglecting to incorporate a large number of factors that can affect the effort needed on a project. For this reason, practitioners and researchers have turned to algorithmic methods to estimate effort.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

