  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": " producer guidance: if you are a producer of reusable components, keep the component general by using parameters and anticipate similar conditions; separate dependencies so that changing pieces are isolated from those that remain the same.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "On the other hand, if you are a producer of reusable components, you should keep in mind several things:\n - Make your component general, using parameters and anticipating similar conditions to the ones in which your system will invoke your components.\n - Separate dependencies so that sections likely to need change are isolated from those that are likely to remain the same.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Strategy and concepts: reuse can be viewed in terms of classification, retrieval, and repository use, with approaches that are either compositional or generative.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Reuse Technology and Component Retrieval. One of the biggest obstacles to reuse is the need to search through a large set of software products to find the best one for a particular need.The job is akin to sifting through a junkyard of books rather than visiting a library.The solution is component classification , where collections of reusable components are organized and catalogued according to a classification scheme.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Approaches to reuse are either compositional or generative. Compositional reuse views reusable components as a set of building blocks, and development is done from the bottom up,building the remaining system around the available reusable components. For this kind of reuse, the components are stored in a library (often called the reuse repository ). The components must be classified or catalogued, and a retrieval system must be used to scan and select components for use in a new system.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Examples and rationale: a repository can contain building blocks like a time-format converter or a DBMS generator; object-oriented design and language features (e.g., Ada) support reuse.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "For example, our repository might contain a routine to convert the time from 12- to 24-hour format (i.e., so that 9:54 P.M. becomes 2154 hours). Similarly, the Genesis  database-management-system generator includes building blocks that allow us to construct a DBMS tailored to our needs (Prieto-Díaz 1993).",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Another advance that encourages reuse is the increasing sophistication of tools that stretch their effects across the development life cycle. Tools that include a repository for the components developed for  one  system can encourage reuse of those components in a later system. Finally, language design can encourage component reuse. For example,Ada provides software restructuring mechanisms that support packaging, and its parameterization techniques can help to make software more general.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "There should be a direct correspondence between the program design components and the program code components, so that the design's modularity is carried forward into the code and enabling traceability from design to code and back again.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The most critical standard is the need for a direct correspondence between the program design components and the program code components.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Design characteristics, such as low coupling, high cohesion, and well-defined interfaces, should also be program characteristics, so that the algorithms, functions, interfaces, and data structures can be traced easily from design to code and back again.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Keeping design and implementation in sync matters because the system’s general purpose may stay the same while its specific features evolve; changes are made first to the high-level design and then traced down to the code that must be modified.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Remember  that  the  system's  general  purpose  is  likely  to  remain  the  same throughout the software's lifetime, but its nature may change over time as customers identify enhancements and modifications.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "These changes are made first to the high-level design and then are traced through lower design levels to the code that must be modified.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Thus, the correspondence between design and code is essential.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Interfaces should be specified precisely enough to define acceptable implementations, while avoiding overly restrictive constraints that would exclude valid alternatives; this helps prevent developers from coupling to implementation details and maintains maintainability.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Ideally, a unit's interface specification defines exactly the set of acceptable implementations.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Interface specifications keep other developers from knowing about and exploiting our design decisions.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "If a developer writes code that depends on how our software is designed, then the interface between that developer's code and our code has changed: the developer's code now requires more from our software than what is advertised in our software's interface specification.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Design by contract provides a precise interface for interaction, describing preconditions, postconditions, and invariants to form testable, substitutable units that can be replaced with new implementations adhering to the same interface.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "In design by contract , each module has an interface specification that precisely describes what the module is supposed to do.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "This specification, called a contract , governs how the module is to interact with other modules and systems.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Such specification cannot guarantee a module's correctness, but it forms a clear and consistent basis for testing and verification.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Together, these contract properties are called assertions.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "a software unit can be replaced by any new implementation that adheres to the unit's interface specification.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "The reuse you described is consumer reuse (using them in subsequent systems) and, if you modified it, clear-box reuse; if you used it without modification, black-box reuse.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "There are two kinds of reuse, as determined by the perspective of the reuser. Producer reuse creates reusable components, and consumer reuse uses them in subsequent systems (Bollinger and Pfleeger 1990).As consumers, we can either use a product whole, without modification (called black-box reuse ), or modify it to fit our particular needs (called clear-box reuse ).",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Problems you might encounter include the need to verify that a reused module performs its required function, and ensuring that, if it is code, it is failure-free.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "An important issue in black-box reuse is our ability to verify that a reused module performs its required function in an acceptable way. If the component is code, we want to be sure it is failure-free; if it is a test set, we want to be sure it thoroughly exercises the function for which it is intended.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "How these problems can be mitigated or resolved, per the sources, includes revisiting and potentially revising the design (not patching) and reexamining how data, algorithms, and structure are used in the code; and applying reuse-focused guidelines such as making reuse goals measurable, anticipating conflicts between goals, and performing domain analysis to identify commonality.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Revising and Rewriting, Not Patching. When writing code, as when preparing a term paper or creating a work of art, you often write a rough draft. Then you carefully revise and rewrite until you are satisfied with the result. If you find the control flow convoluted, or the decision processes hard to understand, or the unconditional branches difficult to eliminate, then it may be time to return to the design. Reexamine the design to see whether the problems you are encountering are inherent in the design or in your translation to code. Look again at how the data are represented and structured, at the algorithms chosen, and at the decomposition.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Reuse goals should be measurable, so that we can tell if we have met them.",
                "img_id":  null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Different perspectives may generate different questions. Whereas programmers ask specific technical questions, such as 'Is this sort routine written in C ++ ?' a vice-president is focused on the corporation as a whole and its position in the marketplace, asking, 'Are we saving money yet?'",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "An underlying activity in both kinds of reuse is domain analysis , the process of analyzing an application domain to identify areas of commonality and ways to describe it",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    }
]

