                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Statement testing: Every statement in the component is executed at least once in some test.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Test case 1: Input X that follows a path with a single straightforward transformation leading to Y = f1(X).",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In our example, statement testing requires fewer test cases than branch testing, which in turn requires fewer cases than path testing.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Test case 2: Input X that exercises an alternate path causing a different transformation and a second assignment to Y.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In our example, statement testing requires fewer test cases than branch testing, which in turn requires fewer cases than path testing.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "All-uses testing: include at least one path from every definition to every use that can be reached by that definition.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "All-uses testing: The test set includes at least one path from every definition to every use that can be reached by that definition.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Stronger vs weaker strategies and test-case requirements:",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "We are likely to do better with a strategy than with random testing. For example, Ntafos (1984) compared random testing with branch testing and all-uses testing on seven mathematical programs with known faults. He found that random testing found 79.5% of the faults, branch testing found 85.5%, and all-uses testing found 90%.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "There are many other test strategies that can be employed during unit testing. For example, secure applications are often tested by following each possible transaction to its end, employing a strategy called transaction flow testing .",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Testing all paths is stronger than testing all paths from definition to use.",
