[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Cleanroom aims to certify software with respect to the specifications and to produce zero-fault or near-zero-fault software, applying a structured process that emphasizes early omission detection and formal correctness concepts.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The principles are applied by blending several techniques discussed in this and earlier chapters. First, software is specified using box structures, introduced in Chapter 8. The system is defined as a black box, refined as a state box, and refined again as a clear box. The box structures encourage analysts to find omissions in requirements early in the life cycle, when they are easier and cheaper to fix.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "to certify the software with respect to the specifications, rather than wait for unit testing to find the faults",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "to produce zero-fault or near-zero-fault software",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet_intro",
    "segment": "Principles and techniques highlighted in Cleanroom include:",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The box structures encourage analysts to find omissions in requirements early in the life cycle, when they are easier and cheaper to fix.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "box structures and formal progression from black box to state box to clear box, with a correctness theorem governing intended functions",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The system is defined as a black box, refined as a state box, and refined again as a clear box.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "A correctness theorem defines a relationship, expressed as one of three correctness conditions, that describes the correctness of each intended function with respect to its control structures.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Differences from conventional testing include organizational and methodological shifts such as separating development and test activities, relying on peer review, using informal design abstractions, and basing testing on operational scenarios rather than unit tests alone.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "- separation of the development and test teams",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "- reliance on peer review instead of unit testing",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "- use of informal state machines and functions to define the system design",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "- statistical testing based on operational scenarios",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "In practice, Cleanroom also emphasizes iterative learning and tailoring to the environment, with evidence that results vary by project size and context, and cautions from researchers about extrapolating results across different environments.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The SEL's cleanroom experience teaches us several things. First, Basili and Green (1994) have shown us how to use a combination of experiments and case studies to compare a new technique with an existing one.They tailored both the technique and the investigative process to the organization involved and the results of previous studies.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The final step involves statistical usage testing, where test cases are randomized based on probability of usage, and this feeds into a quality model to estimate measures such as the expected mean time to failure, contrasting with conventional coverage testing.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The final step involves statistical usage testing, where test cases are randomized based on probability of usage, as we saw earlier in this chapter.The results are used in a quality model to determine the expected mean time to failure and other quality measures.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Cobb and Mills (1990) report that statistical usage testing is more than 20 times as effective at extending MTTF than is coverage testing.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The Ariane-5 failure could have been exposed by tests that validate each level of the system, including equipment testing, on-board computer software testing, staged integration, and system validation tests. The investigation notes that the flight control system was tested in four ways: - equipment testing - on-board computer software testing - staged integration - system validation tests.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The flight control system was tested in four ways:\n - equipment testing\n - on-board computer software testing\n - staged integration\n - system validation tests",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "However, there was no test to verify that the SRI would behave correctly under countdown and flight time sequence and the trajectory of Ariane-5, meaning a critical gap where the failure could have been observed earlier.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "no test was performed to verify that the SRI would  behave  correctly  when  being  subjected  to  the count-down  and  flight  time sequence and the trajectory of Ariane-5",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The investigators argued that such a declaration of limitation, which should be mandatory for every mission-critical device, would have served to identify any non-compliance with the trajectory of Ariane-5, highlighting the importance of ensuring requirements explicitly cover mission-specific variations.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Such a declaration of limitation, which should be mandatory for  every mission-critical device, would have served to identify any non-compliance with the trajectory of Ariane-5.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The root cause was in the requirements, so reviewing and validating requirements and test coverage earlier in development might have revealed inadequacies; the investigators concluded that the limitations of the SRI software were not fully analyzed in reviews, and the test coverage was inadequate to expose such limitations.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Because the root cause is in the requirements, it could have been noticed quite early  during  the  development  process. Indeed, reviews  were  an  integral  part  of  the design and coding activities, and the investigators point out that they were 'carried out at all levels and involved all major partners in the project (as well as external experts).'",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Resources available to users: formal documentation accompanies every system and supports training, with manuals or online documents accessible to users as the system functions; icons and online help are provided to make functions clear and easy to understand, and training can be delivered in various forms to ensure information is always accessible.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Documents. Formal  documentation  accompanies  every  system  and  supports training.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The system manuals are often similar to an automobile owner's manual; they are references to be used when a problem or question arises.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Icons and Online Help. A system can be designed so that its interface with the user makes system functions clear and easy to understand.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Training can be done in many ways. No matter how training is provided, it must offer information to users and operators at all times, not just when the system is first delivered.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Resources available to operators: operator manuals explain hardware and software configurations, methods for granting and denying access, procedures for adding or removing peripherals, and techniques for duplicating or backing up files; operator training emphasizes familiarity with the system’s support functions and covers how to bring up and run the system as well as how to support users.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Operator's  manuals  present  material  to  operators  in  the  same  fashion  as  user's  manuals.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Thus, the operator's manual explains hardware and software configurations, methods for granting and denying access to a user, procedures for adding or removing peripherals from a system, and techniques for duplicating or backing up files and documents.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Operator Training. The focus of operator training is familiarity with the system's support functions; this training addresses how the system works rather than what the system does.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Operators are trained on two levels: how to bring up and run the new system, and how to support users.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

