[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "For feasibility in the organization you’d start with a collection of case studies (or field studies) to evaluate whether the software reading technology is feasible in the environment.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Thus, if only 51% of the evidence is in the plaintiff's favor, then the jury must find for the plaintiff.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "This  board  game  and  its  associated  investigative  pitfalls  offer  us  guidelines  in making decisions about software technology. But software engineering does not always present easily controlled situations, so we must be realistic and practical.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "For testing very specific hypotheses about the technology, use replicated controlled experiments (i.e., controlled experiments that can be replicated).",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "On the other hand, researchers prefer to perform studies involving reproducible validation methods that can be used in isolation in the laboratory, such as theoretical proof, static analysis, and simulation. They discounted methods that required interacting  directly  with  practitioners. In  other  words, most  researchers  shunned  the  very methods that are most valued by practitioners: case studies, field studies, and experiments.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "replicated controlled experiments",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "For examining the use of the technology in detail, use in-depth studies that look at how it is used within actual environments.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Some studies looked at whether or not the technology was feasible in the environment, other studies tested very specific hypotheses about the technology, and still other studies examined the use of the technology in detail.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Sketch of a series of studies for organization Z (incorporating all three goals):",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Some studies looked at whether or not the technology was feasible in the environment, other studies tested very specific hypotheses about the technology, and still other studies examined the use of the technology in detail.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Proposed sequence: (1) start with case studies/field studies to establish feasibility within Organization Z's environment; (2) follow with replicated controlled experiments to test specific hypotheses about the technology; (3) conclude with in-depth or longitudinal studies to examine detailed use and integration.",
        "citations": [
            {
