[
    {
        "role": "bullet_intro",
        "segment": "To draw meaningful and accurate conclusions about design inspections, John can consider the following.",
        "citations": []
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Focus on preparation rate rather than team size: the preparation rate, not the team size, determines inspection effectiveness.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "He found that a three-person inspection team with a lower preparation rate did as well as a four-person team with a higher rate; he suggested that the preparation rate, not the team size, determines inspection effectiveness.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Increase familiarity with the product since the more familiarity there is, the better the inspection effectiveness tends to be.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "the more familiarity, the better.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Be aware that inspections and tests may identify different fault types; inspections tend to find coding or low-level design faults, while testing can reveal requirements and architectural faults.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "inspections began with the code. But the requirements continued to evolve during unit and integration testing, and the code size almost doubled during that time. Comparing the code inspection data with the test data, Weller found that code inspections identified mostly coding or low-level design faults, but testing discovered mostly  requirements and architectural faults.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "Consider adjusting inspection rate and monitoring fault density: evidence suggests slower inspections (below 125 LOC/hour) found more faults, and organizations can tailor inspection processes to improve fault detection.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "In the second project, the inspections with rates below 125 found an average of 46% more faults per thousands of lines of code than those with faster rates. This finding means either that more faults can be found when inspection rates are slower or that finding more faults causes the inspection rate to slow.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "The information allows AT&T to tailor its inspection process so that more faults are found and products are thereby improved.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

